    <div class="gp-sec" aria-label="Article introduction"><div class="gp-wrap"><div class="gp-byline"><img class="gp-avatar" src="data:image/webp;base64," alt="Marcus Hale" width="132" height="132" loading="lazy"> Written by Marcus Hale on August 9, 2026 <span class="gp-ad">Advertisement</span></div><h1>Doctors Call It &ldquo;The 1% Decline.&rdquo; Most Men Call It Getting Old. Here&rsquo;s What&rsquo;s Actually Happening After 35.</h1><div class="gp-hero"><img src="data:image/webp;base64," alt="Alpha Brew Coffee" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" width="900" height="1062"></div><p class="gp-lede">It doesn't happen overnight. That's the problem.</p><p>You don't wake up one day feeling different. It creeps. The gym sessions that stopped feeling intense. The 9pm tiredness that used to be midnight tiredness. The drive &mdash; for work, for the gym, for her &mdash; that quietly dialed itself down while you weren't watching.</p><p>I'm 43. I assumed all of it was just the deal. It isn't.</p><p><mark>Starting in your mid-30s, men lose roughly 1% of their testosterone every year.</mark> Slow enough that you never notice a single week of it. Fast enough that by 45, you're running on 10&ndash;15% less of the hormone that makes you feel like <em>you</em>.</p></div>
